# RG35XX RC1 exact-source integration spec: MobilePlatform dirty-frame scheduling
# Task: RGJ-RC1-008
# Owner remains org.recompile.mobile.MobilePlatform.
# Helper remains the registered org.recompile.mobile.RG35XXFrameScheduler.
# Do NOT create another frame scheduler, repaint thread, framebuffer owner or MobilePlatform replacement.

Target: upstream FreeJ2ME-Plus devel src/org/recompile/mobile/MobilePlatform.java
Helper: src/org/recompile/mobile/RG35XXFrameScheduler.java

## Exact-source findings

Current upstream MobilePlatform owns both LCD buffers (`lcd`, `lcdFrontbuffer`), their PlatformGraphics
contexts, `painter`/`postDraw`, frame-limit timing state, input dispatch and LCD resize. Therefore RG35XX
must attach dirty signalling to the authoritative point where a newly rendered/backbuffer result becomes
available to the libretro presentation path. It must not add a second rendering loop.

The current RG35XXFrameScheduler is generation based:
- markDirty() increments generation and notifyAll();
- waitForChange(lastSeen) blocks until generation changes;
- wake() only wakes waiters and does not fabricate a new frame;
- reset() returns generation to 1 and intentionally makes the first post-reset frame eligible.

## Integration contract

1. `MobilePlatform` remains the sole Java owner of LCD/frontbuffer mutation and repaint semantics.
2. On RG35XX/libretro only, call `RG35XXFrameScheduler.markDirty()` after the existing upstream render /
   backbuffer-to-frontbuffer operation has completed and the frame is coherent for the native video sender.
3. Do NOT call markDirty() at the beginning of paint, before copy/flush, or for every primitive draw call.
   A dirty generation represents a presentable frame, not arbitrary pixel mutation.
4. LCD resize/recreation must force a dirty generation after the new buffers/graphics references are fully
   installed, so the native side cannot continue presenting stale geometry.
5. Pause/menu/overlay transitions that already invoke the upstream painter may mark dirty only after that
   painter has produced the visible state. Do not introduce a polling repaint loop.
6. Game unload/reset uses `RG35XXFrameScheduler.reset()` through RG35XXLifecycle. Final shutdown may use
   `wake()` only to release a waiting frame worker; wake is not a rendered frame.
7. The consumer stores its last-seen generation and calls waitForChange(lastSeen). Multiple producer marks
   may coalesce naturally; this is intentional and prevents a queue of obsolete frames.
8. No per-frame object allocation, Timer, executor, java.util.concurrent queue or additional Java thread is
   introduced by this gate.
9. Existing upstream frame limiting (`lastRenderTime`, `requiredFrametime`, `LockSupport`) remains authoritative.
   RG35XXFrameScheduler is a dirty/wakeup signal, not another FPS limiter.
10. Preserve `lcd`/`lcdFrontbuffer`, `painter`, `postDraw`, GameCanvas semantics, DoJa paths and existing
    front-end callbacks. Do not move framebuffer ownership into RG35XXFrameScheduler.

## Java-runtime compatibility gate

RG35XX project-owned helper code remains Java-6 syntax/API compatible. Current upstream MobilePlatform imports
`java.util.concurrent.locks.LockSupport`; this is an upstream exact-source compatibility item to verify during
consolidated JamVM build. This task does not add any new `java.util.concurrent` dependency.

## Duplicate/missing-source result

No new Java class/package is required. Authoritative pair:
- `MobilePlatform` = rendering/buffer/repaint owner.
- `RG35XXFrameScheduler` = generation-based dirty/wakeup helper only.

## Gate status

STATIC-AUDIT-PASS for ownership and integration contract.
BUILD-PASS is forbidden until the exact presentation call-site is applied in the consolidated source and the
JamVM/Ant build succeeds. DEVICE-TEST-PASS remains separate.
